<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tihama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tihamah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tihamah is the hot coastal plain along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a> to the Bab el-Mandeb strait. Its southern part, in Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, holds ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jizan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a> and was farmed and traded across the sea for centuries. In the early 20th century the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a> ruled from towns on the plain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tihama.jpg" alt="Tihama" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran_tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_regions_of_Arabia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
